  "query_preview": "..\n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nUpper level ridging across the central CONUS will allow for a \npersistent southwesterly flow aloft across the Northern Rockies this \nweek. Wildfire smoke from Oregon, Washington and vicinity will more \nor less continue to be ushered into the region through the week, \nresulting in continued hazy/smoky skies. Temperatures do look to \nslowly trend warmer through the week, though there is some \nuncertainty as to how quickly the trend develops. \n\nWeak waves progressing through the southwesterly flow aloft will \nyield the risk for a few afternoon showers and thunderstorms most \ndays this week, mainly east of I-15 across Southwest Montana. These \nlooks to produce little in way of precipitation, however.\n\nThe heat looks to peak on Saturday immediately ahead of the breaking \ndown of the upper level ridge. As the ridge breaks down late \nSaturday into Sunday, breezy winds look to develop in most areas. \nThis ridge breakdown looks to be completely dry, which raises \nconcerns for fire weather, should the windier scenario unfold. -AM\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nTemperatures this week:\n\nWildfire smoke will play a large role in just how quickly \ntemperatures trend warmer this week across the region. Regardless, \ntemperatures will be above average during the climatologically \nhottest portion of the year, which is notable. Those working \noutside this week should take extra care to mitigate impacts from \nboth the heat and wildfire smoke. \n\nRidge breakdown this weekend:\n\nConfidence remains high that a ridge breakdown will occur this \nweekend. Temperatures immediately ahead of this ridge breakdown look \nto trend even further above average Saturday before falling \nSunday as breezier westerly winds ensue. The uncertainty with this\nsystem will be for the timing of the stronger winds aloft. They \npresently look to be timed to occur overnight, which would result \nin a slightly less breezy day (But certainly still breezy) into \nSunday. Should the stronger winds aloft be delayed slightly, \nSunday would trend much breezier. -AM",
